Yes. Based on the screenshots, the cleanest approach is:

1. Add address to CustomerFacilitiesDTO.


2. Create a separate buildAddress() method.


3. While mapping customerFacilities, get the corresponding FacilityDetails from facilityDetailsMap using facilityId.


4. Build the address from address1, address2, city, state, zip.


5. Keep the address populated even when isLocationActive() returns false. The active flag should only indicate whether the location is active; it should not affect whether its address is returned.



1. Modify CustomerFacilitiesDTO

Your current DTO:

public class CustomerFacilitiesDTO {
    private String id;
    private String facilityId;
    private String name;
    private Boolean active;
}

Change it to:

public class CustomerFacilitiesDTO {
    private String id;
    private String facilityId;
    private String name;
    private Boolean active;
    private String address;
}

Lombok @Data and @Builder will automatically handle the new field.


---

2. Modify the mapping in getAllLocationsFromPreferenceDB()

You currently have essentially:

customerFacilities = customerFacilities.stream()
        .map(CustomerFacilitiesDTO data -> CustomerFacilitiesDTO.builder()
                .id(data.getId())
                .facilityId(data.getFacilityId())
                .name(data.getName())
                .active(isLocationActive(
                        facilityDetailsMap,
                        billingGroupDetailsMap,
                        data.getFacilityId(),
                        billingGroupId,
                        customerId))
                .build())
        .sorted(Comparator.comparing(CustomerFacilitiesDTO::getName))
        .toList();

Change it to:

customerFacilities = customerFacilities.stream()
        .map(data -> {
            var facilityDetails = facilityDetailsMap.get(data.getFacilityId());

            return CustomerFacilitiesDTO.builder()
                    .id(data.getId())
                    .facilityId(data.getFacilityId())
                    .name(data.getName())
                    .active(isLocationActive(
                            facilityDetailsMap,
                            billingGroupDetailsMap,
                            data.getFacilityId(),
                            billingGroupId,
                            customerId))
                    .address(buildAddress(facilityDetails))
                    .build();
        })
        .sorted(Comparator.comparing(CustomerFacilitiesDTO::getName))
        .toList();

The important part is:

var facilityDetails = facilityDetailsMap.get(data.getFacilityId());

and then:

.address(buildAddress(facilityDetails))

This happens independently of isLocationActive().

So even if:

isLocationActive(...) == false

the response will still contain:

{
  "id": "...",
  "facilityId": "...",
  "name": "...",
  "active": false,
  "address": "..."
}


---

3. Add separate buildAddress() method

Since your FacilityDetails has:

private String address1;
private String address2;
private String city;
private String state;
private String zip;

I'd recommend building the address while safely ignoring null/empty fields:

private String buildAddress(CustomerHierarchyResponse.FacilityDetails facilityDetails) {

    if (facilityDetails == null) {
        return "";
    }

    return Stream.of(
            facilityDetails.getAddress1(),
            facilityDetails.getAddress2(),
            facilityDetails.getCity(),
            facilityDetails.getState(),
            facilityDetails.getZip())
            .filter(value -> value != null && !value.isBlank())
            .collect(Collectors.joining(", "));
}

You'll need:

import java.util.stream.Collectors;

If Stream is already imported in your class, you're good.


---

4. Why this also handles inactive/filtered locations

Your current isLocationActive() does this:

var isActive = false;

var facilityDetails = activeFacilities.get(facilityId);

if (facilityDetails != null && Boolean.TRUE.equals(facilityDetails.getActive())) {
    ...
}

return isActive;

So if the facility isn't active, it returns:

false

But it doesn't need to be changed for the address requirement.

The address comes directly from:

facilityDetailsMap.get(data.getFacilityId())

while active comes from:

isLocationActive(...)

Therefore they are independent:

Customer Facility
       |
       +---- facilityId
       |
       +----> facilityDetailsMap
       |          |
       |          +-- address1
       |          +-- address2
       |          +-- city
       |          +-- state
       |          +-- zip
       |                    |
       |                    v
       |              buildAddress()
       |                    |
       |                    v
       |                 address
       |
       +----> isLocationActive()
                    |
                    v
                 active

One important point

If by "filtered out in isLocationActive" you mean that some locations are actually being removed from the customerFacilities list somewhere else based on active, then that filtering needs to be removed.

For example, don't do this:

.filter(CustomerFacilitiesDTO::getActive)

because that would completely remove inactive locations from the response.

Instead, return them with:

"active": false

and still include their:

"address": "Address 1, Address 2, City, State, ZIP"

So the final response can contain both active and inactive locations, with the inactive ones clearly marked by active: false.